hmm, so I'd prefer this could be moved to some helper function in src/basic/fs-util.[ch], as we use posix_fallocate() at two other places, and they should take benefit of this logic too. maybe call this posix_fallocate_loop().

I think just counting down is not going to work though, if we really are concerned about busy looping. iirc strace will generate EINTR when attaching and when detaching, hence in particular if you pick 2 you already hit it. 

My suggestion: let's use the ratelimit logic we already have for this. Specifically something like this:

```c
int posix_fallocate_loop(int fd, uint64_t offset, uint64_t size) {
        Ratelimit rl;
        int r;

        r = posix_fallocate(fd, offset, size); /* returns positive errnos on error */
        if (r != EINTR)
                return -r; /* Let's return negative errnos, like common in our codebase */

        /* On EINTR try a couple of times more, but protect against busy looping (not more than 16 times per 10s) */
        rl = (RateLimit) { 10 * USEC_PER_SEC, 16 };        
        while (ratelimit_below(&rl)) {
                r = posix_fallocate(fd, offset, size);
                if (r != EINTR)
                        return -r;
        }
        return -EINTR;
}
```

(yeah, I'd fix up the error semantics to be more like our usual ones, i.e. return negative errnos. And I think it would be wise to always try posix_fallocate() once without preparing the ratelimit stuff, since that requires querying the clock. Also, off_t sucks because noone knows that that is, hence I'd fix the offset/size params to be uint64_t)

